n, or were imprecise near the clinical boundary. The accepted claim set therefore remains empty. These conclusions are dataset-specific associations only; they do not establish causality or generalizability.\n\nThe most informative next step, if further analysis were funded, would be to avoid spending validation on borderline discovery-only signals and instead conduct prespecified, adequately powered screens of untested predictors or clinically motivated interactions with a strict prior requirement that the registered-direction interval exclude zero and the raw magnitude reach the clinical scale. With current evidence, the scientifically defensible conclusion is that no tested predictor or subgroup is reliably associated with a clinically meaningful difference in these outcomes.",
